%MACHINE_TRANSLATED% Article 44-2 (Korea Forest Soil and Stone Association) (1) The Korea Forest Soil and Stone Association shall have a secretariat and expert committees. <Amended on Mar. 11, 2025> (2) The Korea Forest Soil and Stone Association shall have a chairperson, vice-chairperson, directors, and auditors as executives. <Amended on Mar. 11, 2025> (3) Matters necessary for the business of the Korea Forest Soil and Stone Association, the number, term, and election method of executives, qualifications of members, establishment of branches, etc. shall be prescribed by the articles of association. <Amended on Mar. 11, 2025> %MACHINE_TRANSLATED% Article 44-2 (4) The Korea Forest Soil and Stone Association shall prepare and submit the following documents to the head of the Korea Forest Service by the end of February each year: <Amended on Mar. 11, 2025>
1. Business performance report and financial statement for the previous year;
2. Business plan and income and expenditure budget for the current year.

%MACHINE_TRANSLATED% Article 45 (Prevention of Disasters, etc.) Article 45 (1) In order to conduct investigations, inspections, or examinations under Article 37 (1) of the Act, the head of the Korea Forest Service, etc. may take the following actions against persons engaging in forest conversion, temporary use of mountainous districts, extraction of soil and stone (hereinafter referred to as "forest conversion, etc."), or restoration: <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Oct. 30, 2018>
1. Request for reports;
2. Request for submission of materials;
3. Access to the mountainous district;
4. Any other action deemed necessary by the head of the Korea Forest Service to prevent forest disasters or preserve the landscape of mountainous districts. (2) Under the first sentence of Article 37, paragraph (2) of the Act, "inspection institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the Mountainous Districts Conservation Association and the Korea Erosion Control Technology Association (hereinafter referred to as "inspection institutions" in paragraphs (3) and (5)). <Newly Inserted on Jun. 2, 2020; Aug. 19, 2020; Dec. 16, 2021> (3) Inspection institutions shall conduct investigations, inspections, examinations, etc. (hereinafter referred to as "investigations, etc.") by means of document review or on-site inspection under the first sentence of Article 37, paragraph (2) of the Act. <Newly Inserted on Jun. 2, 2020; Aug. 19, 2020> (4) Investigations, etc. shall be conducted at least once every year based on the construction commencement date, from the construction commencement date of new and renewable energy facilities under the New and Renewable Energy Development, Use and Dissemination Promotion Act until 3 years after reporting the commencement of business under Article 9, paragraph (4) of the Electric Business Act. <Newly Inserted on Jun. 2, 2020> (5) Inspection institutions that have been requested to conduct investigations, etc. under the first sentence of Article 37, paragraph (2) of the Act shall submit the results of investigations, etc. to persons who received forest land conversion permits or temporary use permits for mountainous districts within 30 days from the completion of investigations, etc., and persons who received forest land conversion permits or temporary use permits for mountainous districts shall submit the results to the head of the Korea Forest Service, etc. within 7 days from receiving the results. <Newly Inserted on Jun. 2, 2020; Aug. 19, 2020> (6) The management plan for solar power generation facilities under Article 2, paragraph (2)(a) of the New and Renewable Energy Development, Use and Dissemination Promotion Act (hereinafter referred to as "mountainous district solar power generation facilities") under Article 37, paragraph (3) of the Act shall include the following matters: <Newly Inserted on Aug. 19, 2020> 1. Status of installation and management of mountainous district solar power generation facilities; 2. Status of disaster occurrence and forest damage due to installation of mountainous district solar power generation facilities; 3. Disaster prevention measures and mountainous district restoration plans (including management plans for mountainous district solar power generation facilities); 4. Implementation and inspection plans for mountainous district solar power generation facility management plans; 5. Other matters deemed necessary by the head of the Korea Forest Service, etc. for disaster prevention and mountainous district restoration. (7) Under Article 37, paragraph (6) of the Act, "mountainous district specialized institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" means the Mountainous Districts Conservation Association and the Korea Erosion Control Technology Association. <Newly Inserted on Dec. 16, 2021> (8) When the head of the Korea Forest Service, etc. orders necessary measures under the main text of Article 37, paragraph (7) of the Act, they shall specifically determine the content and period of measures and notify in writing. <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Jun. 2, 2020; Aug. 19, 2020; Dec. 16, 2021> (9) The head of the Korea Forest Service, etc. shall require re-deposit under Article 38 of the Act for restoration expenses reduced by the cost of proxy restoration under Article 37, paragraph (9) of the Act. <Amended on Aug. 22, 2012; Jun. 2, 2020; Aug. 19, 2020; Dec. 16, 2021>

%MACHINE_TRANSLATED% Article 16 (Rules on Management of Weapons) (1) A petitioner who has borrowed weapons and ammunition under Article 16 of the Decree shall manage such weapons and ammunition in accordance with the following subparagraphs: <Amended on Dec. 31, 2020; Dec. 31, 2021> 1. When a petitioner borrows weapons and ammunition, they shall maintain and record weapons and ammunition ledgers and weapon equipment operation cards as prescribed by the Commissioner General of the Korean National Police Agency; 2. The petitioner shall designate a management supervisor for the management of weapons and ammunition and notify the competent chief of police station of such fact; 3. Armories and ammunition storage facilities shall be installed on a single floor and equipped with ventilation, moisture-proof, fireproof facilities, and gun racks; 4. Ammunition storage facilities shall be installed separately from armories, and their location shall be isolated from offices or other facilities that accommodate many people or where many people come and go; 5. Armories and ammunition storage facilities shall have double locking devices, and keys shall be kept by the management supervisor, but shall be transferred to and kept by the duty supervisor after working hours; 6. The petitioner shall assess the management status of weapons and ammunition monthly as prescribed by the Commissioner General of the Korean National Police Agency and notify the competent chief of police station by the 3rd day of the following month; 7. When accidents such as loss, theft, robbery, or damage of borrowed weapons and ammunition occur, the petitioner shall immediately notify the competent chief of police station of the reasons; 8. When weapons and ammunition are lost, stolen, robbed, or damaged, the petitioner shall compensate for the full amount as prescribed by the Commissioner General of the Korean National Police Agency: Provided, That this shall not apply when the Commissioner of Provincial Police Agency recognizes that there were unavoidable circumstances such as war, emergency, natural disasters, or other force majeure. (2) Where the head of an organization who has borrowed weapons and ammunition pursuant to Article 16 of the Decree intends to issue or collect such weapons and ammunition to or from auxiliary police officers, the following requirements shall be observed: Provided, That the number of rounds of ammunition under subparagraph 2 may be increased or decreased, issuance or collection of weapons and ammunition may be suspended, and weapons and ammunition may be recalled and centrally managed, in accordance with instructions of the competent chief of the police station:
1. When weapons and ammunition are issued or collected, the issuance or collection details shall be recorded in the weapons and ammunition ledger;
2. Ammunition shall be issued within the limit of 15 rounds per rifle and 7 rounds per pistol, and in such cases, older ammunition (by production date) shall be issued first;
3. Weapons and ammunition issued to auxiliary police officers shall be cleaned at least once a week;
4. If a weapon requires repair, a repair request may be made to the competent chief of the police station, along with a list of such weapons and the relevant weapon management card. (3) Auxiliary police officers who have been issued weapons and ammunition by the petitioner shall observe the following matters: 1. When receiving, returning, or transferring weapons, they shall perform "inspection arms" from the "port arms" position; 2. When issued weapons and ammunition, unless otherwise instructed, they shall carry weapons and ammunition separately, maintain the "sling arms" position for rifles, and maintain the "holster pistol" position for pistols; 3. Issued weapons may not be kept or carried by others, nor may maintenance be requested from others; 4. When cleaning or handling weapons, the muzzle shall always be pointed toward the air; 5. When returning weapons and ammunition, maintenance shall be thorough; 6. After working hours, weapons and ammunition shall be returned to the petitioner or transferred to relief duty personnel. (4) The petitioner shall not issue weapons and ammunition to auxiliary police officers who fall under any of the following subparagraphs, and shall immediately retrieve any weapons and ammunition that have been issued: <Amended on Dec. 31, 2021; Nov. 10, 2022> 1. A person who has become subject to disciplinary action for misconduct in duties; 2. A person who has become subject to investigation for criminal matters; 3. A person who has expressed intention to resign; 4. A person who is recognized by a specialist in the relevant field as inappropriate for carrying weapons and ammunition due to mental illnesses such as dementia, schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, bipolar disorder (manic-depressive disorder), or recurrent depressive disorder; 5. A person whom the petitioner recognizes as inappropriate for issuing weapons and ammunition for reasons similar to those in subparagraphs 1 through 4; 6. Deleted <Nov. 10, 2022>. (5) When not issuing or retrieving weapons and ammunition under paragraph (4), the petitioner shall prepare a decision notice in Form No. 5-2 attached hereto and notify the relevant auxiliary police officer without delay: Provided, That when prompt retrieval of issued weapons and ammunition is deemed necessary, weapons and ammunition may be retrieved first and the notice may be given afterward. <Newly Inserted on Nov. 10, 2022> (6) When the petitioner does not issue or retrieves weapons and ammunition from auxiliary police officers under paragraph (4), they shall prepare and notify the competent chief of police station with a decision notification in Form No. 5-3 attached hereto within 7 days. <Newly Inserted on Nov. 10, 2022> (7) Article 16 (7) Upon receiving a notification under paragraph (6), the competent chief of the police station may, within 14 days from the date of receipt, visit the site, hear the opinion of the relevant auxiliary police officer, and take necessary measures to determine the appropriateness of restricting or recalling the issuance of weapons and ammunition. <Newly Inserted on Nov. 10, 2022> (8) When the reasons under each subparagraph of paragraph (4) are resolved, the petitioner may issue weapons and ammunition to auxiliary police officers. <Newly Inserted on Nov. 10, 2022>

%MACHINE_TRANSLATED% Article 15 (1) Medical personnel or founders of medical institutions shall preserve medical records, etc. as referred to in Article 22 (2) of the Act for the periods specified in each of the following subparagraphs. Provided, That if necessary for continued treatment, such periods may be extended once within the limits of the respective periods specified below: <Amended on May 29, 2015; Oct. 6, 2016; Dec. 29, 2016>
1. Patient register: 5 years;
2. Medical records: 10 years;
3. Prescriptions: 2 years;
4. Surgical records: 10 years;
5. Test data and written findings: 5 years;
6. Radiographic images (including visual media) and written opinions: 5 years;
7. Nursing records: 5 years;
8. Midwifery records: 5 years;
9. Copies of medical certificates, etc. (to be preserved separately by type, such as medical certificates, death certificates, and postmortem examination reports): 3 years. (2) The medical records referred to in paragraph (1) may be preserved in their original form using microfilm, optical discs, or other similar media (hereinafter referred to in this Article as "film"). (3) When preserving medical treatment records by the method under paragraph (2), the person responsible for film photography shall write the photography date and time and their name on the film cover, and sign or affix a seal.

%MACHINE_TRANSLATED% Article 106 (Merger of Investment Trusts) (1) An asset management company may merge investment trusts by absorbing the investment trust property of other investment trusts managed by the same asset management company. Article 106 (2) Where an asset management company intends to merge investment trusts, it shall prepare a merger plan stating the following matters and obtain approval from the general meeting of beneficiaries of each investment trust subject to the merger: <Amended on Oct. 5, 2004>
1. The amount of the increased trust principal and the number of beneficiary certificates of the surviving investment trust resulting from the merger;
2. Matters concerning the allocation of beneficiary certificates to be issued to the beneficiaries of the dissolved investment trust;
3. Where cash is to be paid to the beneficiaries of the dissolved investment trust, the details thereof;
4. The date of the general meeting of beneficiaries of each investment trust to be merged;
5. The date on which the merger is to take place;
6. Where the trust deed of the surviving investment trust is to be amended due to the merger, the details thereof;
7. Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree. (3) The main contents of the merger plan shall be stated in the convocation notice for the general meeting of beneficiaries. (4) The provisions of Article 527-5, paragraphs (1) and (3) of the Commercial Act shall apply mutatis mutandis when an investment trust with creditors merges. In this case, "company" shall be deemed "asset management company" and "general meeting of shareholders" shall be deemed "general meeting of beneficiaries." <Amended on Jul. 19, 2007> (5) Necessary matters concerning the merger of investment trusts, such as the method for calculating the merger value of beneficiary certificates, shall be prescribed by Presidential Decree.

%MACHINE_TRANSLATED% Article 185 Any person who falls under any of the following subparagraphs shall be punished by a fine not exceeding five million won: <Amended on Oct. 5, 2004; Jul. 19, 2007>
1. A person who establishes or amends trust deeds without submitting the report under the proviso to Article 29 (1) or Article 31 (1);
2. A person who makes a false or fraudulent report under the proviso to Article 29 (1) or Article 31 (1);
3. A person who fails to report the establishment details of an investment trust in violation of Article 29 (3) or who files a false report;
4. A person who violates Article 86 (3) or (4);
5. A person who transfers invested equity in violation of Article 144-14 (2);
6. A person who fails to report or makes a false report in violation of Article 144-16 (3) or (4);
7. A person who fails to file a report or makes a false report in violation of Article 144-17 (2);
8. A person who engages in business after being suspended under Article 57 (1) of the Securities and Exchange Act, as applied mutatis mutandis under Article 148;
9. A person who violates Article 175 (3) by transferring indirect investment securities to another person;
10. A person who delegates business in violation of Article 176 (1);
11. A person who violates Article 177.
